data.content:
## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

**Pattern identified:** Timeout enforcement — aborting a thread that exceeds a time limit.

**Why it breaks:** `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone. The replacement is cooperative cancellation with `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter`.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Usage Change

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework)
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(() => ComputeExpensiveResult(), timeout);

// After (.NET 8) — work must accept and check the token
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    // Check token at regular intervals in long-running work
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        // ... do work ...
    }
    return finalResult;
}, timeout);
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| **Work ignores the token** — cancellation never fires | Insert `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints inside the lambda. Cooperative cancellation only works if the code checks. |
| **Blocking calls (I/O, Sleep) don't respond to the token** | Replace `Thread.Sleep(ms)` with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)`. Use async I/O overloads that accept `CancellationToken`. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. Leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use the `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to distinguish timeout from external cancellation. |
| **Expecting immediate termination like `Thread.Abort`** | Cancellation is not preemptive. If work has long gaps between checks, it won't stop instantly. Design checkpoint frequency accordingly. |
| **Uncooperative third-party code** | If you can't modify the work to check a token, move it to a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill()` on timeout. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort`. |

---

### If You Need a Backward-Compatible Overload

```csharp
// Convenience overload for simple delegates (still cooperative — just less granular)
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    return ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
    {
        // WARNING: cancellation only checked before/after — not during work()
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        var result = work();
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        return result;
    }, timeout);
}
```

⚠️ This overload **cannot** interrupt `work()` mid-execution — it only checks before and after. For true timeout behavior the work itself must cooperate with the token.
